One improvement I would make

After designing this, I would actually merge the Intent and Alias agents into the search object of every concept instead of storing them as separate concept types.

For example, a Right to Refund card would already contain:

"search": {
  "keywords": [
    "refund",
    "replacement",
    "compensation"
  ],
  "aliases": [
    "money back",
    "return my money"
  ],
  "user_queries": [
    "Amazon won't refund me",
    "Seller refused refund",
    "Can I get my money back?"
  ]
}

This keeps all retrieval signals attached to the legal concept itself. The vector search, keyword search, and hybrid retrieval all operate on the same object, which is cleaner than maintaining separate "intent" or "alias" databases that can drift out of sync with the underlying legal knowledge.

If I were building this from scratch today, this is the schema I would freeze before writing a single line of RAG code. It cleanly separates immutable law (V1) from human-readable legal knowledge (V2) while keeping V2 flexible enough to support chat, search, document generation, and future knowledge graph expansion.